24.5-2 Convicted person not eligible to act as authorised representative

Any person convicted by a court of an offence involving fraud shall not be eligible to act as authorised representative for a period of 10 years from the date of such conviction.

Officer who is in default

The expression "officer who is in default" under the Companies Act means officers who could be held liable for the default committed by the company in complying with the provisions of the Act and is defined to mean all or any of the following officers of the company for the purpose of any provision of the Act which enacts that an officer of the company who is in default shall be liable to be punished -

The definition mentions the following types of "officer who is in default";

(i) those who hold some defined position, such as managing director, whole-time director, manager;

(ii) any director other than (i) above in respect of contravention which had taken place with this consent or connivance or is attributable to any neglect on his part or the director or officer charged by the board with the responsibility of complying with the provision;

(iii) "shadow directors" in accordance with whose directions the board of directors are accustomed to act;

(iv) an employee receiving remuneration more than of the whole-time managing director or whole-time director and who himself or along with his spouse or dependent children holds not less than two per cent of the equity share capital;

(v) in the absence of any of the above, any director or directors specified by the board in this behalf and where none so specified, then all directors, the secretary, the chief accounts officer (in respect of offences for which he is specifically responsible), the share transfer agents, bankers, registrars to the issue, merchant bankers (in respect of the issue or transfer of any securities of the company).

Company is a legal person, a fiction of law which does not exist in actuality. The issue of liability of the officers through whom the company acts is an imputed liability. The liability is imputed to the high managerial officers. The person is liable because his action is the very action of the company itself and is charged as a person in charge and responsible to the conduct of the company’s business, and not in individual capacity. The person is not liable independently of his character as being a person in charge. For example, the managing director of the company from his very designation implies both control and command of the affairs of the company and equally a statutory liability to the company for its business [see Rajagopalchari v. Bellary Spinning and Weaving Co. Ltd. [1997] 90 Comp. Cas. 485 (Ker.)]. It is compendious term which signifies both control and responsibility to the company both in the ordinary parlance and by virtue of the provisions of the Companies Act. Similarly in the case of manager, from the very nature of his duties, it can safely be inferred that he would undoubtedly be vicariously liable for the offence, vicarious liability being an incident of an offence (see Municipal Corporation of Delhi v. Ram Kishan Rohtagi AIR 1983 SC 67)

As in the case of torts, the general rule prevails that the corporation may be criminally liable for the acts of an officer or agent, assumed to have been done by him while exercising authorized powers, and without proof that that his act was expressly authorized or approved by the corporation. In case of strict liability guilt shall not be predicated upon the automatic breach of the statute but on the establishment of the actus reus, subject to defence of due diligence. Whereas in the case of the absolute liability, it arises instantaneously upon the breach of the statutory prohibition, no particular state of mind is a prerequisite to guilt. Whether strict or absolute, depends upon the terminology used in the provision. Corporations and individuals are placed on the same footing in the face of a statutory offence [see Standard Chartered Bank v. Directorate of Enforcement [2005] 275 ITR 81 (SC) at p. 96]. The omission or commission of the statutory breach is itself the offence. Strict liability means liability without fault or mens rea. Absolute offences are not criminal offences in any real sense [see J. K. Industries v. Chief Inspector of Factories & Boilers [1997] 88 Comp. Cas. 285 (SC)].

